{
  "id": "Compound::DB01222 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Budesonide, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of asthma through its interaction with glucocorticoid receptors. This relationship is supported by its inclusion in the corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist class, which is known to modulate inflammatory responses by suppressing the activity of immune cells and reducing airway inflammation. The evidence from multiple paths indicates that Budesonide, through its action on glucocorticoid receptors, exerts a therapeutic effect on asthma, as evidenced by its ability to treat asthma in the context of corticosteroid hormone receptor agonists, including fluticasone propionate, hydrocortisone, and betamethasone. The shared lowest common ancestor, 21-hydroxy steroid, suggests a common biological pathway involving steroid hormone metabolism and receptor activation. The integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Budesonide and asthma, emphasizing the role of glucocorticoid receptor signaling in modulating inflammatory processes. The consistency across different corticosteroid receptor agonists and their therapeutic effects on asthma underscores the biological relevance of this relationship, though further studies are needed to fully elucidate the precise mechanisms and clinical implications.",
